|~ `::error`, the kind of every error, the refusal of an error whose
   path is no vector of stops, and the verbs of errors. Each resides on `::qlang/error`, the kind
   every error is a value of: the error `!|` opens is `::error{…}`, or
   the tag of the site that refused stacked over it, and its walk passes
   `::error` on its way to the map, so a verb declared here reaches every
   error. ~|

::error
  |~~ The kind of errors. A reader learning a tool fails often, and
      a failure is the moment when the knowledge it needs is cheapest to
      give, so a failure is a value that carries it: the facts of the
      failure, the path it took, and the tag of the site that refused,
      which names the page of the procedure. An error is a map of facts
      under `::error`, `::error{:k 1}`, a value like any other until it
      is raised, and the bang of a literal raises it, `!{:k 1}`. Every
      refusal is a tag of its own, named for what failed,
      `::AddLeftNotNumberError`, stacked over the error. `|` passes a
      raised error by, `*` stops on one, and `!|` opens it to that
      stack, so `!| type` reads the site, the verbs of this kind reach
      every error, and every step works on it as on any value: a fact is
      added and the error raised again, or it is recovered by the tag of
      its site. ::snippet~(::error | spec | /verbs) lists its verbs.
      ::link~(::explanation | doc) ::link~(::fail | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang | doc :errors)

      ~(!{:detail "boom"} !| type | eq ::error)
      ~(::qlang/error | eq ::error)
      ~("x" | add 1 !| type | eq ::error | not)
      ~("x" | add 1 !| payload | type | eq ::error)
      ~(::error{:k 1} | /k | eq 1)
      ~("x" | add 1 !| union {:request "r-1"} | raise !| /request | eq "r-1")
      ~("x" | add 1 !| if (type | eq ::AddLeftNotNumberError) ~(0) ~(raise) | eq 0)
      ~("x" | add 1 !| if (type | eq ::Other) ~(0) ~(raise) !| type | eq ::AddLeftNotNumberError)
  ~~|

::ErrorTrailNotVecError
  |~~ `:trail` on an error descriptor is the path of the error, a
      vector of stops the runtime writes, `{:step :subject :skipped}`,
      from the step where it arose to the step where it is read.
      A literal or a re-lift that writes a value that is no
      vector under `:trail` fires this error at mint time. A re-lift
      that writes the `:trail` it read resumes that path, and one that
      leaves it out starts a path at the step that lifts it.
      ::link~(::error | doc)

      ~(
        !{:kind :oops :trail 5}
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::ErrorTrailNotVecError ::number]
       )
      ~(
        !{:kind :oops} | count
        !| union {:trail 5} | raise
        !| type | eq ::ErrorTrailNotVecError
       )
      ~(
        !{:kind :oops} | count
        !| minus #[:trail] | raise | add 1
        !| /trail * /step | eq [~(raise)]
       )
  ~~|

:trail
  |~~ The path of the error: each step it passed, from the one
      that raised it, with the subject that step met and the steps
      skipped after it.

      ~("x" | add 1 !| trail | count | eq 1)
      ~("x" | add 1 | mul 2 !| trail | first | /skipped | eq ~(mul 2))
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:returns ::vec | /trail)
